## Quick Start

### Using a tape image (no physical drive needed)

```bash
# 1. Start the daemon (in the background or a separate terminal)
./ltape startdaemon        # or: ./ltaped &

# 2. Assign a device path to a short letter.
#    For file-backed mode, use any non-existent path prefix.
#    ltaped will create <path>.p0.dat and <path>.p1.dat automatically.
./ltape assign /tmp/mytape a

# 3. Read the LTFS index from the tape into the disk cache.
#    For a fresh file-backed path this also formats a new empty tape.
#    Use -m instead of -f to keep the working index in daemon memory.
./ltape indexread -f a

# 4a. Copy files straight to/from tape without mounting.
./ltape push ./myfile.txt a:/docs/myfile.txt
./ltape ls   a:/docs
./ltape get  a:/docs/myfile.txt ./restored.txt
./ltape rm   a:/docs/myfile.txt

# 4b. …or mount it as a filesystem (default: file-backed temp dir).
mkdir -p /mnt/tape
./ltape mount a /mnt/tape
# Or with an in-memory temp dir:  ./ltape mount -m a /mnt/tape
cp myfile.txt /mnt/tape/
ls /mnt/tape/
./ltape flush a            # push the staged temp files to tape now
./ltape umount a

# 5. Commit: write the updated index back to the tape image
./ltape commitindex a

# 6. Stop the daemon
./ltape killdaemon
```

### Using a real / MHVTL tape drive

```bash
./ltaped &

./ltape assign /dev/nst0 a   # ltaped detects the char device automatically
./ltape indexread -f a       # reads the index into the disk cache
./ltape push ./bigfile.iso a:/bigfile.iso
./ltape commitindex a        # flush the working index to tape
```

