## Command Reference

All commands are sent to `ltaped` via the Unix socket at
`/tmp/ltape/ltaped.sock` (except `startdaemon` / `killdaemon`, which are handled
locally by the `ltape` client).  Commands support **unambiguous prefix
abbreviation**: type enough letters to uniquely identify a command.
An ambiguous prefix prints all matches; e.g. `ltape d` prints
`ambiguous: "d" matches defrag, discardindex`.

A tape address has the form `<letter>:/path/on/tape` (the path defaults to `/`
when omitted).

```
ltape <command> [args]

  startdaemon                  Fork ltaped into the background.
  killdaemon                   Signal the running ltaped to stop.

  assign  <device> <letter>    Register a device under a single-character letter.
                                <device> may be:
                                  • A path prefix for file-backed images, e.g.
                                    /tmp/tape1  (creates /tmp/tape1.p0.dat and
                                    /tmp/tape1.p1.dat)
                                  • A no-rewind SCSI tape device, e.g. /dev/nst0

  indexread [-m|-f] <letter>   Open the tape and read the LTFS index from block 3
                                of the index partition.  For a fresh file-backed
                                path the tape is formatted first.
                                  -f  cache the index on disk at
                                      /tmp/ltape/index/<letter>.index (default)
                                  -m  keep the working index in daemon memory

  ls  <letter>:/path           List a directory (or a single file) on tape.

  rm [-r] <letter>:/path       Remove a file, or a directory tree with -r,
                                marking freed tape ranges as available space in
                                the index (contiguous holes are merged).

  get <letter>:/path [dest]    Read a file or directory tree directly from tape
                                and write it to the local filesystem (default
                                destination: the current directory).

  push <local> <letter>:/path  Write a local file or directory tree onto tape,
                                updating the index and overwriting any existing
                                target.

  mount [-m|-f] <letter> <dir> Mount the tape as a FUSE filesystem.  The daemon
                                creates <dir> if it does not exist.  Requires
                                indexread to have been run first.
                                  -f  file-backed temp dir in /tmp/ltape/buffer/
                                      (default)
                                  -m  in-memory (heap) temp dir

  umount   <letter>            Unmount the FUSE filesystem.

  flush    <letter>            Push all staged files in the mount temp dir to
                                tape immediately, without waiting for file
                                descriptors to be closed.

  commitindex   <letter>       Write the working index (from memory or the disk
                                cache) back to the tape (rewind to block 3,
                                overwrite, write double filemark).  Increments the
                                generation number.

  discardindex  <letter>       Close the tape partitions and drop the working
                                index without writing to tape.  Use this to
                                abandon uncommitted changes.

  defrag   <letter>            Currently a no-op stub: logs the request and
                                returns.
```

### Flushing inside the mount

Writing any data to the special file `/.tapefuse_flush` inside the mount point
triggers an immediate index flush to tape (equivalent to `ltape commitindex`):

```bash
echo flush > /mnt/tape/.tapefuse_flush
```

---

## Normal Workflow Summary

```
assign → indexread → (push/get/ls/rm  or  mount → use FS → flush → umount)
       → commitindex → …
```

After `mount`, file data written through the FUSE filesystem is staged in the
temp dir and written to tape when each file is closed (or on `ltape flush`).
The directory index (filenames, sizes, timestamps, extent locations) lives in
the working index and must be written back with `ltape commitindex` or by
writing to `/.tapefuse_flush`.

---

## Tape Layout

tapefuse uses a single sequential layout for both backends.  Every
block, including filemarks, is assigned a consecutive block number.

```
Block 0   Data   VOL1 label (80-byte ANSI label with barcode)
Block 1   Data   LTFS label (XML, partition map + volumeUUID)
Block 2   FM     Filemark  — end of label area
Block 3   Data   LTFS index (XML)  ← always rewritten here on commit
Block 4   FM     Filemark
Block 5   EOD    End-of-data marker for the index region / start of data region
Block 6   Data   First file's data
Block 7   FM     Filemark
Block 8   EOD    End-of-data marker (removed and rewritten on each append)
…
```

Each file write appends `[Data, FM, EOD]`.  On the next write the EOD is
removed (TruncateAt) before the new `[Data, FM, EOD]` is appended, so the
final on-tape layout is `[Data₁, FM, Data₂, FM, …, Dataₙ, FM, EOD]`.

For **file-backed images** this layout is split across two files:

| File | Covers |
|------|--------|
| `<device>.p0.dat` | Blocks 0–5 (index partition) |
| `<device>.p1.dat` | Blocks 6+ (data partition, local numbering starts at 0) |

For **SCSI tape devices** the entire layout lives on a single tape.  The
`SCSIRegionTape` wrapper translates between the local block numbers used by the
higher-level code and global tape positions.

---

## Index Management

The LTFS index is an XML document that records the complete directory tree,
per-file metadata, and the location of every file's data on tape (expressed
as a list of extents: partition, startBlock, byteOffset, byteCount).

tapefuse keeps the working index either in the disk cache or in daemon memory:

1. `indexread -f` reads the index XML from tape and writes it to
   `/tmp/ltape/index/<letter>.index`; `indexread -m` keeps it in daemon memory.
2. Every FUSE metadata operation (Attr, Lookup, ReadDir, Create, Mkdir,
   Remove, Rename) reads the disk file, modifies it in memory, and writes it
   back atomically (write to `.tmp`, then `rename`).
3. `commitindex` reads the working index, calls `TapeFS.FlushIndex`, which
   rewinds to block 3, overwrites the old index, writes two filemarks, and
   increments the generation number.  The updated index is also written back.
4. `discardindex` simply drops the working index (deleting the disk cache
   file).

File **data** never passes through the index cache.  On each `write(2)` the
`FileHandle.Write` FUSE callback stages the data in the mount's temp dir
(memory- or file-backed); when the file is closed (or on `ltape flush`) the
staged content is written to the data partition as a single block via
`TapeFS.WriteFileData`, which reuses a freed slot where possible, and the
resulting extent is recorded in the working index.

