## Design Notes

**Why disk-cached index?**  
Keeping the LTFS index only on tape would require a full tape rewind and
sequential read on every directory listing.  Keeping it only in RAM risks
index loss on daemon crash.  The disk cache gives fast access while keeping
the RAM footprint minimal: the XML is loaded, modified, and written back as a
single atomic file operation per metadata call.

**Why explicit commit?**  
Tape drives are write-once sequential media.  Appending a new index on every
file write would rapidly consume the tape.  Batching index writes (commit)
matches how both LTFS reference implementations and real tape software behave.

**Why buffer writes per file handle (no immediate tape write)?**  
Tape drives achieve maximum throughput when writing large, contiguous blocks.
The Linux FUSE layer limits each `write(2)` call to 128 KB, so writing
directly on each FUSE write would produce one tape block per chunk — wasting
tape space due to inter-block gaps and preventing the drive from reaching
streaming speed.  Instead, tapefuse accumulates each file's data in an
in-memory buffer and writes the entire file as a single tape block when the
file descriptor is closed.  This is the same strategy used by LTFS and
LTFSCopyGUI.  For existing files, the current tape content is pre-loaded into
the buffer on the first write so that partial overwrites are handled
correctly.

**Why two partition files for the file backend?**  
LTFS on LTO-5+ hardware uses SCSI medium partitions.  The two-file approach
mirrors that structure and simplifies the higher-level `TapeFS` code, which
always addresses the "index partition" and "data partition" independently.

**Why no in-memory index in the FUSE layer?**  
Caching the parsed index in the FUSE layer would create a coherency problem:
`commitindex` reads the disk cache, and `FileHandle.Write` writes to it.  If the
FUSE layer held a separate in-memory copy, a crash between a Write and a
Commit could produce an index that referred to data blocks that were never
actually written.  The disk-IO cost per metadata operation is negligible
compared to tape seek latency.
